WEBVTT

1
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:29.988
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOC's course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.497
We will be continuing our discussion on resource optimization.

3
00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:50.541
In particular, we are talking about resource optimization from the point of view of rendering, which is a very important component in any extended reality applications including

4
00:00:52.006 --> 00:00:54.550
virtual reality and augmented reality applications.

5
00:00:58.295 --> 00:01:13.438
So, we will primarily focus on immersive experience, as we have already stated earlier, and we are going to relate the discussion to VR resource optimization in particular.

6
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.528
Although such concerns equally apply to AR,

7
00:01:21.571 --> 00:01:33.933
However, the emphasis is more on resource optimization in the context of VR because realistic scene generation is more important there and that

7.1
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:37.616
requires more resources as compared to AR experiences.

8
00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:45.047
In order to understand resource optimization concerns, we started our discussion on

9
00:01:45.517 --> 00:01:55.528
computer graphics, which deals with the rendering process, rendering on a computer screen or any display device such as HMDs.

10
00:01:57.911 --> 00:02:04.118
So, we learned about the basic idea of computer graphics, including the 3D graphics pipeline stages.

11
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:13.228
Also, so far we have covered three of those stages, namely the first stage, object representation; the second stage,

12
00:02:14.238 --> 00:02:26.656
modeling transformation; and the third stage, lighting calculation or calculation of intensities at the pixel point pixel positions.

13
00:02:26.676 --> 00:02:37.572
In this part of the lecture, we are going to talk about the fourth stage of the pipeline, that is, view transformation.

14
00:02:37.592 --> 00:02:43.080
So, just to put things into perspective, we have this 3D graphics pipeline.

15
00:02:51.683 --> 00:02:57.889
Now, here we have object representation, the first stage, which takes place in the local coordinate reference frame.

16
00:02:58.589 --> 00:02:59.550
We already covered it.

17
00:03:00.191 --> 00:03:05.377
Then we have modeling transformation, the second stage, also known as geometric transformation, which

17.1
00:03:05.377 --> 00:03:16.046
transfers the object definition from local coordinates to the definition in a world coordinate scene.

18
00:03:16.066 --> 00:03:21.471
Then we have the third stage, that is lighting calculation, which

19
00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:25.557
as per our pipeline discussion, takes place in the world coordinate scene.

20
00:03:27.578 --> 00:03:35.105
Now, this lighting calculation is quite complex, and we talked about one simple lighting model.

21
00:03:37.948 --> 00:03:49.298
In this part, today's part of the lecture, we are going to cover the fourth stage, that is the viewing pipeline, comprising all these transformations and other processes.

22
00:03:51.758 --> 00:03:54.121
And later on, we will cover the scan conversion.

23
00:03:57.084 --> 00:04:03.651
So, let us get the idea of the fourth stage, that is view transformation.

24
00:04:04.813 --> 00:04:10.659
Here again, our emphasis will be on giving you the basic idea rather than going into the intricate details.

25
00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:17.867
So, we will try to confine our discussion to a bare minimum level.

26
00:04:20.411 --> 00:04:21.713
So, what is the basic idea?

27
00:04:23.995 --> 00:04:35.367
The idea is that when we see an image on a screen, any screen including HMD, we are basically seeing a projection of a portion of the 3D scene.

28
00:04:36.047 --> 00:04:42.193
So, we have a 3D scene which we have constructed and illuminated with our earlier stages.

29
00:04:42.814 --> 00:04:50.301
Now, when we say that we are trying to render it on a computer screen, then essentially what we are doing is

30
00:04:50.838 --> 00:05:02.057
Selecting a portion of it and then projecting it from a 3D scene to a 2D display plane or 2D view plane.

31
00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:08.287
So, this is similar to taking a photograph.

32
00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:15.759
So, if you are a photographer or if you know something about capturing photos, what do we do?

33
00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.402
We typically have a camera.

34
00:05:17.855 --> 00:05:20.039
Any camera, even your smartphone camera.

35
00:05:20.439 --> 00:05:26.930
So, when we try to capture a photo, first we look at the scene with our naked eye; that is the 3D scene.

36
00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:32.459
Then we look at the same scene using the camera screen.

37
00:05:36.005 --> 00:05:39.170
Then we capture a portion of the scene.

38
00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.816
So, the scene, our field of view covers a wide range of

39
00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:44.888
a wide portion of the scene.

40
00:05:45.049 --> 00:05:52.156
However, we do not capture the entire thing; we capture only the portion which is within the field of view of the camera.

41
00:05:53.177 --> 00:05:58.702
And we set the camera in such a way that our area of interest is captured.

42
00:06:00.183 --> 00:06:04.688
Now, when we say captured, that means we are able to see it on our screen.

43
00:06:04.708 --> 00:06:12.195
Now, the screen is a 2D plane, whereas the scene, the portion of the scene that we have captured, is in the 3D space.

44
00:06:12.867 --> 00:06:24.179
So, then there is a transformation, a projection transformation, which transforms from 3D space to a 2D screen.

45
00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:32.948
The same thing we try to simulate in computer graphics with a set of stages, which are the viewing pipeline stages.

46
00:06:37.132 --> 00:06:40.596
So, view transformation essentially is this viewing pipeline.

47
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:53.616
It has its own sub-stages that simulate the process of capturing a photo.

48
00:06:53.636 --> 00:07:09.016
So first, we do a viewing transformation, a 3D viewing transformation, where the 3D world coordinate description of the scene gets transformed to a 3D view coordinate description.

49
00:07:09.036 --> 00:07:10.718
So we define a view coordinate system.

50
00:07:13.853 --> 00:07:15.895
So, this is a 3D view coordinate.

51
00:07:16.336 --> 00:07:25.407
So, we define a view coordinate system, then perform a transformation operation that transforms the 3D world coordinates into the view coordinate system.

52
00:07:25.467 --> 00:07:30.894
Now, this is akin to saying that we are looking at the scene through the camera lens.

53
00:07:32.215 --> 00:07:42.848
When we capture a photo, before that, we see the scene using the camera mechanism, the camera viewing mechanism provided.

54
00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:52.728
So, looking at the scene through the camera viewing mechanism and looking at the scene through our naked eyes, these two are different things.

55
00:07:53.789 --> 00:08:02.342
And when we are looking at the scene through the camera viewing mechanism, essentially the scene gets transformed from the world coordinate system to

55.1
00:08:02.342 --> 00:08:08.591
a view coordinate system that is defined by the camera or, in other words, the camera parameters.

56
00:08:08.711 --> 00:08:10.253
So, that is what happens here.

57
00:08:11.735 --> 00:08:22.633
The second thing is a projection transformation that projects the 3D view coordinate scene to a view plane, which is a 2D plane.

58
00:08:26.019 --> 00:08:35.193
So, this is the projection transformation from the 3D view coordinate system to the 2D view coordinate system or 2D view plane.

59
00:08:37.368 --> 00:08:42.832
And then finally, there is another transformation, which is the window-to-viewport transformation, that happens here.

59.1
00:08:42.832 --> 00:08:57.974
Here, we transform from a 2D view plane description of the scene, or the projected scene, to a 2D device coordinate space.

60
00:09:00.917 --> 00:09:07.105
Now, here, there are two more operations that take place: one is clipping, and one is hidden surface removal.

61
00:09:07.928 --> 00:09:19.464
Clipping takes place in the 3D view coordinate system, as per our way of understanding the pipeline, and hidden surface removal again takes place in the 3D view coordinate system.

62
00:09:20.165 --> 00:09:30.339
Just to emphasize here is that the way we are explaining the pipeline, it is not necessary that this sequence should be followed in implementation.

63
00:09:30.379 --> 00:09:37.108
So, different implementations can alter the sequence of the stages or sub-stages.

64
00:09:37.527 --> 00:09:46.356
So, this sequence of stages or sub-stages is not sacrosanct; it may be changed depending on the implementation.

65
00:09:46.376 --> 00:10:01.751
So, we have three transformations: viewing transformation, projection transformation, and window-to-viewport transformation; and two operations: clipping and hidden surface removal.

66
00:10:03.252 --> 00:10:06.235
Together, they constitute the viewing pipeline.

67
00:10:09.151 --> 00:10:12.374
That is the fourth stage of the 3D graphics pipeline.

68
00:10:15.057 --> 00:10:18.600
Now, let us try to understand these transformations in brief.

69
00:10:20.542 --> 00:10:29.992
So, let us start with the first one, which is the 3D viewing transformation, where we transform from a 3D world coordinate description to a 3D view coordinate description.

70
00:10:30.952 --> 00:10:35.597
This is to simulate the camera photo-taking analogy,

71
00:10:37.737 --> 00:10:43.546
looking at the same scene, not with our bare eyes, but with the camera viewing mechanism.

72
00:10:44.828 --> 00:10:51.678
So, first, we need to define the view coordinate system, and then we perform the transformation.

73
00:10:52.199 --> 00:10:58.568
And this definition of the view coordinate system depends on the parameters, the camera parameters.

74
00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:03.095
So, the setup is something like this.

75
00:11:03.175 --> 00:11:06.360
So, this is the point in the world coordinate scene.

76
00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:18.793
This is the world coordinate system reference frame within which all the scene points are defined.

77
00:11:19.334 --> 00:11:38.203
Now, we have to define a view coordinate system having its own axes u, n, and v. So, essentially what we are trying to do is transfer this point in the world coordinate

78
00:11:39.162 --> 00:11:45.992
to this view coordinate scene, the 3D view coordinate scene.

79
00:11:46.012 --> 00:11:48.616
So, we are given the camera parameters.

80
00:11:48.857 --> 00:11:49.898
What are those parameters?

81
00:11:50.038 --> 00:12:05.021
The camera position serves as the origin, which can be represented by this origin vector with respect to the world coordinate scene.

82
00:12:05.041 --> 00:12:07.785
So, this is the vector. Then,

83
00:12:08.896 --> 00:12:23.145
Second is the view of point, again, can be another vector, the view of point, and the view direction we can define with respect to the vector. And third is the

83.1
00:12:23.145 --> 00:12:31.180
center of interest or the look-at point P. This is the center of interest or the look-at point.

84
00:12:32.441 --> 00:12:36.726
So, before taking a photo, we typically focus on a look-at point.

85
00:12:37.178 --> 00:12:39.580
or center of interest, and then capture the photo.

86
00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:42.583
So, that idea is taken here.

87
00:12:44.746 --> 00:12:49.390
Now, from these parameters, we can set up the view coordinate system.

88
00:12:50.732 --> 00:12:51.793
We will skip the details.

89
00:12:53.254 --> 00:12:57.819
For those details, you can refer to the material cited at the end of the lecture.

90
00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:06.848
So, we can simply say that to determine the parameters, to determine the view coordinate system, we need to determine the three

91
00:13:07.655 --> 00:13:24.114
orthogonal axes N, V, and U. N can be obtained; N is the unit basis vector opposite to the looking direction and can be obtained with respect to the origin vector and the

91.1
00:13:24.114 --> 00:13:35.738
look-up direction, that is the N vector, and then the unit vector we can obtain by its value.

92
00:13:38.789 --> 00:13:53.432
Then, that is the first axis N. Then, the second one we can determine is the V unit basis vector along the V up direction, that is,

92.1
00:13:53.432 --> 00:14:08.087
we can first get the V up direction, that is V up minus O, and then we get the unit basis vector as this ratio.

93
00:14:09.299 --> 00:14:22.654
Once these two, n and v, are obtained—remember n is obtained using the origin vector and the look-at point, v is obtained using the view up point and

93.1
00:14:22.654 --> 00:14:30.379
the origin point—and once these two are determined, we can determine u, the third

94
00:14:32.772 --> 00:14:44.251
axis and the third unit basis vector, as u simply is the cross product of v and n.

94.1
00:14:44.251 --> 00:14:58.240
So, that is how we can determine the three mutually orthogonal axes, and these three axes together define the view coordinate system.

95
00:15:00.447 --> 00:15:08.464
Now, once we have the view coordinate system, that is the three axes, we can transform the

95.1
00:15:08.464 --> 00:15:20.230
world coordinate description to the view coordinate description by two basic transformation operations: translation and rotation.

96
00:15:21.031 --> 00:15:29.340
So, the composite transformation matrix is the dot product of these two transformation matrices.

97
00:15:30.147 --> 00:15:49.128
r and t, and then any point in the view coordinate system is obtained from the corresponding point in the void coordinate system after the point vector is multiplied with this

97.1
00:15:49.128 --> 00:15:59.850
composite transformation matrix comprising r and t. So, if a point P is here and if we want to transform it to a point in

98
00:16:00.303 --> 00:16:05.289
the view coordinate system, then we can do so using this formulation.

99
00:16:09.033 --> 00:16:25.551
Again here, how to, what is the matrix for M that is the composite matrix, or rather what are the individual matrices for R and T? These details we are skipping.

100
00:16:26.628 --> 00:16:30.232
You can refer to the material cited at the end for those details.

101
00:16:33.176 --> 00:16:34.858
So, that is about view transformation.

102
00:16:34.878 --> 00:16:49.388
So, we define a view coordinate system with respect to three camera parameters, and then we use that information to transform a point from the

102.1
00:16:49.388 --> 00:16:56.365
void coordinate system to the view coordinate system, and those matrices are formed on the basis of those

103
00:16:56.767 --> 00:16:59.411
axis vectors and their scalar components.

104
00:17:04.318 --> 00:17:08.344
Next is projection transformation.

105
00:17:08.364 --> 00:17:17.416
After view transformation, we project 3D objects in the view coordinate system, the 3D view coordinate system, onto a 2D view plane.

106
00:17:20.221 --> 00:17:21.002
So, this is our

107
00:17:23.935 --> 00:17:29.664
world coordinate description; it gets transformed to a 2D view plane description.

108
00:17:31.266 --> 00:17:39.980
So, in between, we first transform it to a 3D view coordinate description, and from there we project it to a 2D view plane description.

109
00:17:40.261 --> 00:17:41.623
So, this is the view plane.

110
00:17:45.088 --> 00:17:50.196
So, such view planes are usually defined as a rectangular area.

111
00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:54.603
which is often called a clipping window.

112
00:17:54.623 --> 00:17:59.308
So, there are different terminologies used to refer to more or less the same concept.

113
00:18:03.691 --> 00:18:20.166
Now, in order to perform projection, apart from the clipping window, we also define a 3D volume, a 3D region in the scene that is basically what portion of the scene

114
00:18:20.905 --> 00:18:22.967
we are going to project.

115
00:18:24.548 --> 00:18:26.110
This is known as the view volume.

116
00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:32.135
So, the view volume defines the portion of the scene that we are going to project on the view plane.

117
00:18:34.637 --> 00:18:44.025
Objects that are inside this view volume are projected on the view plane, and objects that are outside are discarded.

118
00:18:44.045 --> 00:18:50.811
Now, when we say that we are discarding objects that are outside of this view volume but part of the scene,

119
00:18:51.567 --> 00:18:53.810
we call that a clipping process.

120
00:18:56.474 --> 00:19:02.242
So, clipping takes place, as per our description of the pipeline, before we perform the projection.

121
00:19:05.306 --> 00:19:17.743
Now, when we say we want to perform projection, projection can be of different types, and we also have to determine the computation based on the type of projection that we want to have.

122
00:19:18.702 --> 00:19:21.928
So, one type is perspective projection.

123
00:19:22.428 --> 00:19:36.247
Here, the projectors, which are lines emanating from the object in the 3D view coordinate system and intersecting the view plane,

123.1
00:19:36.247 --> 00:19:42.904
these projectors are not parallel to each other; they tend to converge to a center of projection.

124
00:19:44.403 --> 00:19:45.184
Something like this.

125
00:19:45.305 --> 00:19:48.570
So, this is the object, suppose.

126
00:19:50.173 --> 00:19:58.387
Now, if we extend some lines from these vertices of the object, and this is the view plane.

127
00:19:59.148 --> 00:20:09.887
So, they intersect the view plane; the points where they intersect define the projection of that 3D object on the 2D view plane.

128
00:20:10.542 --> 00:20:21.557
In the case of perspective projection, those lines tend to meet at a distance or converge at a distance.

129
00:20:23.119 --> 00:20:28.006
That is called the center of projection or point of projection.

130
00:20:35.937 --> 00:20:38.440
So, this perspective projection gives us

131
00:20:39.112 --> 00:20:43.638
realistic rendering, a realistic feeling.

132
00:20:44.439 --> 00:20:49.886
So, if we want to generate a realistic scene, then we should stick to perspective projection.

133
00:20:51.088 --> 00:20:54.232
Now, there is another type of projection that is called parallel projection.

134
00:20:54.352 --> 00:21:05.507
Here, the projectors are parallel to each other; that is, the center of projection or the point of projection is at infinity, which means they never meet.

135
00:21:06.466 --> 00:21:10.632
This is something like that, as illustrated here.

136
00:21:10.852 --> 00:21:19.265
The idea is something as illustrated in this figure, where we have an object; projectors are shown here.

137
00:21:20.066 --> 00:21:27.136
They intersect the view plane and give us this projection.

138
00:21:27.176 --> 00:21:31.663
However, the projectors are parallel to each other, so they do not meet.

139
00:21:34.782 --> 00:21:42.454
And we say that in such cases, we call that parallel projection, where we say that the projectors meet at infinity.

140
00:21:47.521 --> 00:21:54.460
So, for parallel projection, the view volume, if we want to generate an effect resulting from parallel projection,

140.1
00:21:54.460 --> 00:22:03.405
then we wish to have a view volume that is a rectangular parallelepiped-shaped region as shown here.

141
00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:13.000
And this volume defines the objects that are to be projected on the clipping window.

142
00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:25.360
So, typically such volumes have one face as the clipping window, as shown here, and then we have other faces.

143
00:22:30.132 --> 00:22:34.917
For perspective projection, such a view volume takes the shape of a frustum, as shown here.

144
00:22:35.097 --> 00:22:38.982
Again, one side of the frustum is the clipping window.

145
00:22:39.042 --> 00:22:47.211
Our objective is that whatever objects are within this frustum are to be projected on this clipping window.

146
00:22:51.716 --> 00:22:54.939
Now, there is a concept called a canonical view volume.

147
00:22:55.279 --> 00:22:57.642
So, remember we talked about the clipping operation.

148
00:22:58.178 --> 00:23:02.143
That is, discarding objects that are outside the view volume.

149
00:23:02.784 --> 00:23:18.184
Now, to discard the objects, if we go by the naive method, the simplest most intuitive method, then we have to compute the intersection of the view volume boundaries with the object boundaries.

150
00:23:19.125 --> 00:23:27.055
Now, if we are dealing with arbitrarily shaped view volumes such as the frustums, then typically

151
00:23:27.558 --> 00:23:39.212
calculation of those surfaces and of view volume boundary intersection points turns out to be quite computation intensive.

152
00:23:42.616 --> 00:23:51.387
In order to simplify that, we take recourse to a concept called canonical view volume, which is used to simplify the pipeline computations.

153
00:23:52.608 --> 00:23:53.329
The idea is that

154
00:23:56.448 --> 00:24:06.004
we use a normalized cube to define the view volume. That means whatever view volume is defined,

154.1
00:24:06.004 --> 00:24:15.090
whether for parallel or perspective projection of arbitrary shape and size, we convert those to a normalized cube.

155
00:24:17.173 --> 00:24:26.404
Now, for parallel projection, such a canonical view volume can be obtained by scaling the

156
00:24:28.088 --> 00:24:32.193
arbitrarily defined view volume for the parallel projection.

157
00:24:34.115 --> 00:24:34.976
The idea is shown here.

158
00:24:36.677 --> 00:24:50.793
This is a view volume defined for parallel projection arbitrarily, and this is a transformed view volume which is called the canonical view volume for parallel projection, which is a unit cube.

159
00:24:52.195 --> 00:24:54.417
For perspective projection,

160
00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:58.773
the computation is a little bit higher.

161
00:24:59.474 --> 00:25:10.790
So, here we need to convert the frustum to a parallel canonical view volume, that is, a normalized unit cube.

162
00:25:14.315 --> 00:25:21.805
So, to convert a frustum to a normalized unit cube such as this requires

163
00:25:23.557 --> 00:25:29.684
scaling as well as shearing operations to change the shape along with the change in size.

164
00:25:30.885 --> 00:25:40.716
So, here the amount of computation involved in such a transformation is a bit more as compared to parallel projection.

165
00:25:45.221 --> 00:25:49.786
Now, let us quickly talk about the two projections: how to perform the projections.

166
00:25:49.806 --> 00:25:52.990
Like before, here also we take recourse to

167
00:25:53.678 --> 00:25:56.842
matrices projection transformation matrices.

168
00:25:56.862 --> 00:26:03.209
So, we multiply the point vectors with the transformation matrix to get the requisite projection.

169
00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:07.234
So, for parallel projection we have a matrix like this.

170
00:26:07.494 --> 00:26:20.289
This is a very simplified representation of the matrix where D is the distance of the clipping window from the origin of the 3D view coordinate system.

171
00:26:20.309 --> 00:26:23.112
So, then we have such a representation

172
00:26:24.914 --> 00:26:39.285
in a 4 by 4 matrix and to get a projected point on this clipping window, what we need to do is,

172.1
00:26:39.285 --> 00:26:53.132
we need to have this point vector defined in the 3D view coordinate system, we multiply it with the projection matrix to get this

173
00:26:55.036 --> 00:26:58.940
transformed point in the form of a point vector.

174
00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:05.567
However, here we have this homogeneous factor w which is typically not equal to 1.

175
00:27:07.028 --> 00:27:17.319
So, to get to the Cartesian points what we need to do is basically to divide the obtained coordinate values by this homogeneous factor.

176
00:27:17.339 --> 00:27:19.221
So, to get

177
00:27:20.635 --> 00:27:37.613
Cartesian value along the x direction, we need to divide this corresponding vector value by the homogeneous coordinate, similarly for y and z. We need to do this additional computation here.

178
00:27:41.077 --> 00:27:43.760
What about perspective projection transformation?

179
00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:49.866
So, we have a point here, we want to project it on the clipping window here.

180
00:27:50.858 --> 00:27:55.964
and D is, as usual, the distance of the window from the view coordinate origin.

181
00:27:58.306 --> 00:28:02.891
Here, D is the distance of the window from the center of projection.

182
00:28:02.931 --> 00:28:07.296
Now, the center of projection may typically be assumed to be at the origin.

183
00:28:07.496 --> 00:28:09.358
So, accordingly, the setup can be made.

184
00:28:09.458 --> 00:28:11.881
However, that is not necessarily the case always.

185
00:28:13.102 --> 00:28:19.369
But let us assume that D is the distance from the clipping window to the center of projection.

186
00:28:19.389 --> 00:28:20.410
So, in that case,

187
00:28:20.997 --> 00:28:34.118
we have a perspective projection matrix in this form shown here, which is a bit more complex as compared to the parallel projection matrix.

188
00:28:36.522 --> 00:28:38.105
And then we follow the same procedure.

189
00:28:38.305 --> 00:28:41.390
We have this point vector in the 3D view coordinate system.

190
00:28:42.151 --> 00:28:47.460
We multiply it with the projection matrix, here the perspective projection matrix, to get

191
00:28:48.453 --> 00:29:07.153
the projected point in the homogeneous coordinate form, and then to get to the Cartesian coordinate form we need to divide by the homogeneous factor, as we have done before for parallel projection.

192
00:29:07.173 --> 00:29:08.754
This is for perspective projection.

193
00:29:08.774 --> 00:29:15.962
So, here again, we are skipping many different types of projections under each of these categories, that is, parallel and perspective.

194
00:29:16.735 --> 00:29:22.785
Here, we are just trying to give you a very broad idea of projection and how it can be done.

195
00:29:26.931 --> 00:29:43.738
So, then, let us try to enumerate the sequence of transformations that are involved to project a point P in the world coordinate system to a point on the 2D view plane.

196
00:29:47.161 --> 00:29:53.330
So, first it gets transformed to the view coordinate system, the 3D view coordinate system.

197
00:29:54.632 --> 00:30:06.049
Then the view volume in which the point lies is transformed to a canonical view volume, which is typically the case before we perform the clipping operation.

198
00:30:08.072 --> 00:30:14.822
And finally, the point in the canonical view volume is projected onto the view plane.

199
00:30:15.258 --> 00:30:16.960
through the projection transformation.

200
00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:26.092
So, if P' is the final point that we obtain on the view plane, the 2D view plane, then we can compute P' in terms of the original point P in the world coordinate scene and

200.1
00:30:30.757 --> 00:30:43.554
multiplying P with the transformation matrices for view coordinate, world coordinate to 3D view coordinate transformation matrix, then

201
00:30:43.554 --> 00:30:51.375
a matrix to transform to the canonical view volume, and finally the projection transformation matrix.

202
00:30:54.318 --> 00:31:07.773
So, these are the matrices we need to multiply together to get a composite matrix representing the overall projection from the world coordinate scene to the 2D view plane.

203
00:31:12.018 --> 00:31:14.020
That is up to projection to the view plane.

204
00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:17.887
Now, there is one more projection, which is window to viewport.

205
00:31:18.248 --> 00:31:19.130
Now, what is a window?

206
00:31:20.834 --> 00:31:24.202
A window is the same as a normalized clipping window.

207
00:31:24.222 --> 00:31:28.752
So, typically there is another term for this view plane, which is window.

208
00:31:29.353 --> 00:31:32.059
This is actually a normalized clipping window.

209
00:31:38.182 --> 00:31:49.352
The viewport, on the other hand, is not defined on the view plane; it is defined in the device space, that is, a portion of the final screen where the scene will be rendered.

210
00:31:51.114 --> 00:31:57.620
So, this is defined with respect to the screen origin and the screen dimensions or display origin and display dimensions.

211
00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:02.545
The viewport has nothing to do with the display; it is an abstract intermediate representation.

212
00:32:03.005 --> 00:32:07.429
The window has nothing to do with the display; it is an abstract intermediate representation.

213
00:32:07.898 --> 00:32:16.171
The viewport is defined with respect to the display origin or screen origin and screen dimensions.

214
00:32:16.191 --> 00:32:27.528
So, we need to map from this abstract representation in the window to a device-specific representation in the viewport.

215
00:32:27.548 --> 00:32:35.560
In other words, given a point on the window, we need to transform it to a point on the viewport.

216
00:32:37.261 --> 00:32:48.252
So, if a point is represented in the form W x W y on the window, we want to map it to a point V x V y on the viewport.

217
00:32:48.272 --> 00:32:50.795
So, here I will just talk about the transformation matrix.

218
00:32:51.375 --> 00:32:57.161
I will not dwell on how these matrices or how this matrix is particularly obtained.

219
00:32:57.882 --> 00:33:06.711
That can be obtained by simple geometric operations and correspondence between the two representations.

220
00:33:07.653 --> 00:33:13.541
For the derivation again, I would like to request you to refer to the material cited at the end.

221
00:33:17.086 --> 00:33:36.512
So, the transformation matrix can be represented in the form of this 3 by 3 matrix, where Sx is defined in terms of the viewport dimensions and window dimensions.

222
00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:52.182
Sy is similarly defined with respect to the viewport dimensions and window dimensions along the y-axis; Sx is the dimensions along the x-axis.

223
00:33:52.202 --> 00:34:03.360
Then Tx can be computed in terms of Sx and the window dimension and viewport dimension along x.

224
00:34:04.623 --> 00:34:11.913
Ty can be computed in terms of Sy and again the window and viewport dimensions along y.

224.1
00:34:11.913 --> 00:34:26.766
So, once these are computed, then we can have this transformation matrix T v p, and to get to the point, to get the transformed point, what we need to do is basically multiply

225
00:34:29.833 --> 00:34:39.588
the point with this transformation matrix.

226
00:34:41.891 --> 00:34:47.440
So, we have this point vector transformation matrix; after multiplication, it will yield the transformed point.

227
00:34:50.284 --> 00:34:58.997
So, to get the transformed point, we have this window point W x, W y.

228
00:34:59.399 --> 00:35:14.392
We multiply it with the transformation matrix to get the transformed point as shown here, and to get to the Cartesian points, what we need to do is divide the obtained

228.1
00:35:14.392 --> 00:35:28.798
point with this homogeneous factor W. So, here the representation is again homogeneous coordinate representation, and to get back to the Cartesian point, we need to divide the obtained point with

229
00:35:29.808 --> 00:35:46.991
the homogeneous factor W. Here we should note that there are two more processes that take place in this stage, which we have mentioned earlier: one is clipping, one is hidden surface removal.

230
00:35:47.432 --> 00:35:58.887
So, in clipping, what happens is that we define a view volume; the objects that lie outside the view volume are removed or eliminated. Only the

231
00:35:59.457 --> 00:36:02.440
objects that are inside are retained.

232
00:36:03.481 --> 00:36:17.675
So, it is done before projection because then the projection operations need to be applied on a fewer number of vertices, and the corresponding computational complexity will be diminished or reduced.

233
00:36:17.695 --> 00:36:23.241
So, the idea is illustrated in this figure here.

234
00:36:26.604 --> 00:36:29.447
As you can see, suppose this object; we have this view volume.

235
00:36:29.917 --> 00:36:32.181
This portion of the object is outside.

236
00:36:32.201 --> 00:36:35.746
So, we have to determine this portion of the object and remove it.

237
00:36:36.367 --> 00:36:38.331
In this case, the whole object is outside.

238
00:36:39.613 --> 00:36:47.926
So, we just discard the whole object, whereas in this case the whole object is inside, so we need to retain the whole object.

239
00:36:48.727 --> 00:36:56.059
So, this process of discarding objects, that is, identifying which part to keep and which part to discard, is the clipping process.

240
00:36:59.161 --> 00:37:03.846
Now, another operation that takes place in this stage is hidden surface removal.

241
00:37:05.928 --> 00:37:10.181
Although with clipping we are removing objects that lie outside,

241.1
00:37:10.181 --> 00:37:21.363
sometimes even within the view volume, it is possible that with respect to the viewer position, some portion of the objects or some objects altogether are not visible.

242
00:37:23.205 --> 00:37:25.928
So, in such cases, we need to remove

243
00:37:26.363 --> 00:37:40.464
either fully or partially objects that are inside the view volume, but not visible or not supposed to be visible with respect to a particular viewer position or view position.

244
00:37:40.484 --> 00:37:41.686
The idea is illustrated here.

245
00:37:42.166 --> 00:37:47.915
So, both these objects are within the view volume, and we ideally need to project them.

246
00:37:49.097 --> 00:37:52.742
However, if the viewer is looking at these two objects from this direction,

247
00:37:53.988 --> 00:38:08.642
then you can see that this surface will be occluded by this front object with respect to the viewer or viewing direction.

248
00:38:08.662 --> 00:38:11.485
So, then projecting this surface makes no sense.

249
00:38:12.586 --> 00:38:22.375
So, to further reduce computational complexity, we ideally should remove this surface before projecting the other vertices onto the view plane.

250
00:38:25.578 --> 00:38:38.830
So, this process of identifying objects that are not visible to a viewer or from a particular viewing position is called hidden surface removal.

251
00:38:40.452 --> 00:38:53.003
So, object A is partially blocked from the viewer by B, and if the viewer position changes to this direction, then object

252
00:38:53.219 --> 00:39:06.235
B will be partially blocked by object A. So, for realistic image generation as well as to reduce computation, the blocked portion of A should be eliminated before the scene is rendered.

253
00:39:07.897 --> 00:39:21.353
And in order to do that, we need to remove these surfaces or these surface points from further processing in the pipeline stages. That

254
00:39:21.805 --> 00:39:24.288
is known as hidden surface removal.

255
00:39:24.388 --> 00:39:38.245
That type of removal of surfaces or surface points that ideally should not be rendered with respect to the viewing position is called hidden surface removal.

256
00:39:40.488 --> 00:39:46.075
There are a number of algorithms for each of these operations, namely clipping and hidden surface removal.

257
00:39:47.658 --> 00:39:54.687
They have their own complexities and, of course, introduce more and more computations into the overall process.

258
00:39:55.488 --> 00:40:04.219
However, as is the case with our previous topics, we will skip those here for simplicity.

259
00:40:04.580 --> 00:40:12.110
Our objective is just to give you some idea of the amount of computation involved, not to get into the details of the actual computations.

260
00:40:14.092 --> 00:40:16.956
But for those of you who might be interested in learning more,

261
00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:23.799
please refer to the material cited at the end for more such details.

262
00:40:26.283 --> 00:40:29.928
So, let us summarize what we have learned so far.

263
00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:38.481
So, in this part of the lecture, we covered one more stage of the graphics pipeline, that is the fourth stage, which is the viewing transformation.

264
00:40:39.442 --> 00:40:47.314
Essentially, it is a pipeline in itself involving multiple transformations: view transformation, which transforms

265
00:40:47.682 --> 00:40:58.682
a world coordinate scene to a 3D view coordinate scene; projection transformation, which transforms a 3D view coordinate scene to a 2D view plane description; and finally,

265.1
00:40:58.682 --> 00:41:07.246
viewport transformation, which transforms a 2D view plane description to a 2D device space description.

266
00:41:09.548 --> 00:41:15.816
Also, we mentioned two operations: clipping and hidden surface removal. What are those operations, and what needs to be done

267
00:41:16.353 --> 00:41:18.456
to carry out those operations?

268
00:41:19.177 --> 00:41:26.689
In the next part of the lecture, we will cover the remaining stage of the pipeline, which is the fifth stage: scan conversion.

269
00:41:27.590 --> 00:41:33.780
Along with that, we will have some more discussion on the implications of the knowledge that we have gained so far.

270
00:41:35.122 --> 00:41:42.133
So, I hope that you could follow the topic and understand the topic that we discussed.

271
00:41:42.233 --> 00:41:44.436
Of course, we have skipped a lot of details.

272
00:41:44.922 --> 00:41:48.667
Those details are not relevant for the present course of discussion.

273
00:41:49.067 --> 00:41:58.600
However, for those of you who are more interested, please refer to the material cited at the end of this whole discussion.

274
00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:01.824
So, I look forward to meeting you in the next part of the lecture.

